+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookups resolve against a local embedded SQLite index built from the active snapshot and held only on the kiosk; no service runs to serve lookups and no runtime network access is used. Each kiosk rebuilds its index from each activated snapshot.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot and its index without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active snapshot's SQLite index with the normalized serial. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, rebuilt per kiosk on snapshot activation | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Kiosks have no network route during normal operation and every lookup must complete from files on the kiosk. An on-site HTTPS directory would make lookups depend on the lab network and add service-timeout handling; a managed search service would add recurring cost, credentials, and external connectivity. Both conflict with offline-only operation, so a local index is the only backend that satisfies the constraint. It keeps snapshot validation and index rebuild inside this node, needs no running service, and gives predictable lookup latency. |
